      "prompt_en": "COMPOSITION RULE, HIGHEST PRIORITY: the scene fills the entire 16:9 frame edge to edge as a single full-bleed image — never a letterbox band, never an empty panel, never a flat colour block. The bottom three-eighths of the image height — that is the lowest 37%, roughly the bottom 270 pixels of a 720-pixel-tall frame — is the same continuous scene rendered dark, smoky and free of detail so overlay text stays readable, while all faces, flags, weapons and key objects sit entirely within the upper five-eighths. Anything held or carried sits at mid height, never near the bottom edge. Heavy digital oil painting worked over a photographic base, thick visible brushwork and impasto texture on flags, smoke and cloth, dramatic hard lighting, heavy atmospheric haze, one dominant colour drowning the frame, every part of the upper five-eighths packed edge to edge with elements and no empty space, cinematic wide-angle, very high contrast, painterly and rough rather than clean — a matte-painting finish, NOT a crisp photo composite, NOT a studio cut-out. A North Korean flag — a broad red band with a white circle bearing a red five-pointed star, thin white and blue stripes above and below — fills the entire left half from top to bottom as heavy cloth, and a Russian tricolour of white, blue and red bands fills the entire right half; both are painted with thick visible brushwork, both are sun-bleached and soot-stained with charred edges, and the boundary between them is a standing column of orange flame and black smoke rather than any crack or line. The single central object is a steel border bridge carrying a column of North Korean soldiers out toward the camera, the nearest man waist-up and sharp with his rifle slung, the rest progressively smaller and hazier into the haze. Their faces are lit red from the burning column: the nearest one has his brow furrowed deep, eyes cast down at the deck, lips pressed into a hard line, jaw clenched. no text, no letters, no words, no numbers, no captions, no watermark"
