      "prompt_en": "COMPOSITION RULE, HIGHEST PRIORITY: the scene fills the entire 16:9 frame edge to edge as a single full-bleed image — never a letterbox band, never an empty panel, never a flat colour block. The bottom three-eighths of the image height — that is the lowest 37%, roughly the bottom 270 pixels of a 720-pixel-tall frame — is the same continuous scene rendered dark, smoky and free of detail so overlay text stays readable, while all faces, flags, helmets and key objects sit entirely within the upper five-eighths. Anything held or carried sits at mid height, never near the bottom edge. Heavy digital oil painting worked over a photographic base, thick visible brushwork and impasto texture on flags, smoke and steel, dramatic hard lighting, heavy atmospheric haze, one dominant colour drowning the frame, every part of the upper five-eighths packed edge to edge with elements and no empty space, cinematic wide-angle, very high contrast, painterly and rough rather than clean — a matte-painting finish, NOT a crisp photo composite, NOT a studio cut-out. An enormous Russian tricolour — horizontal white, blue and red bands — hangs across the full width of the background as heavy waterlogged cloth, edges eaten away by burn holes, the folds dull and muddy, harsh side light throwing half of it into deep shadow. The single central object is a heap of empty Russian army helmets half sunk in churned mud at mid height, dozens of them, dented and rust-streaked. Behind the heap a thin line of replacement recruits stands in ill-fitting kit, far fewer of them than of the helmets, receding into brown haze. In the right third, waist-up in front of the flag, a Russian officer in a rain cape presses a palm to his forehead, a reflex he did not mean to make. Eyes squeezed shut, deep vertical line between the brows, mouth open a little as if the breath went out of him, shoulders sunk. A low orange fire glow behind the ridge keeps his face bright. no text, no letters, no words, no numbers, no captions, no watermark"
