      "prompt_en": "COMPOSITION RULE, HIGHEST PRIORITY: the scene fills the entire 16:9 frame edge to edge as a single full-bleed image — never a letterbox band, never an empty panel, never a flat colour block. The bottom three-eighths of the image height — that is the lowest 37%, roughly the bottom 270 pixels of a 720-pixel-tall frame — is the same continuous scene rendered dark, smoky and free of detail so overlay text stays readable, while all faces, flags, structures and key objects sit entirely within the upper five-eighths. Anything held or carried sits at mid height, never near the bottom edge. Heavy digital oil painting worked over a photographic base, thick visible brushwork and impasto texture on flags, smoke and steel, dramatic hard lighting, heavy atmospheric haze, one dominant colour drowning the frame, every part of the upper five-eighths packed edge to edge with elements and no empty space, cinematic wide-angle, very high contrast, painterly and rough rather than clean — a matte-painting finish, NOT a crisp photo composite, NOT a studio cut-out. A vast Russian tricolour — horizontal white, blue and red bands — is strung across the background as heavy cloth, its lower edge already burning, flame eating along the fabric in ragged strips while the three bands stay readable, the whole cloth grimy and desaturated. The single central object is a refinery distillation column caught in the second of collapse, its shell split open and the top third tipping sideways out of a wall of orange flame, pipework peeling away from it. In the left foreground, waist-up, a line of refinery workers in scorched overalls stands watching, the nearest one sharp and the rest hazing out — he grips a wrench because he was still working when it went up. Outer eyebrows sagging, eyes cast up and unfocused, lips parted, soot across his face, fire throwing a hard red key light on him. Orange flame and deep red drown the whole frame, the sky above black with smoke. no text, no letters,