      "prompt_en": "COMPOSITION RULE, HIGHEST PRIORITY: the scene fills the entire 16:9 frame edge to edge as a single full-bleed image — never a letterbox band, never an empty panel, never a flat colour block. The bottom three-eighths of the image height — that is the lowest 37%, roughly the bottom 270 pixels of a 720-pixel-tall frame — is the same continuous scene rendered dark, smoky and free of detail so overlay text stays readable, while all faces, flags, drums and key objects sit entirely within the upper five-eighths. Anything held or carried sits at mid height, never near the bottom edge. Heavy digital oil painting worked over a photographic base, thick visible brushwork and impasto texture on flags, smoke and rusted steel, dramatic hard lighting, heavy atmospheric haze, one dominant colour drowning the frame, every part of the upper five-eighths packed edge to edge with elements and no empty space, cinematic wide-angle, very high contrast, painterly and rough rather than clean — a matte-painting finish, NOT a crisp photo composite, NOT a studio cut-out. A giant Russian tricolour — horizontal white, blue and red bands — covers the background wall of the yard as heavy hanging cloth, charred along the top edge, soot in the folds, the colours dull and muted under raking orange light. The single central object is a mountain of empty fuel drums stacked to the top of the frame, dented and rust-eaten, with one drum tipped on its side at mid height, its bung open and nothing left in it but a thin trail of residue. In the right third, waist-up and standing right in front of the flag, Vladimir Putin (President of Russia) is seen very slightly from above, never from below, upper body only. Eyes cast down at the tipped drum and unfocused, brow furrowed deep, lips pressed into a hard line, shoulders sunk, no hands raised. Distant burning tanks throw a low red glow that keeps his face lit while the rest of the yard falls into smoke. no text, no letters, no words, no numbers, no captions, no watermark"
