      "prompt_en": "COMPOSITION RULE, HIGHEST PRIORITY: the scene fills the entire 16:9 frame edge to edge as a single full-bleed image — never a letterbox band, never an empty panel, never a flat colour block. The bottom three-eighths of the image height — that is the lowest 37%, roughly the bottom 270 pixels of a 720-pixel-tall frame — is the same continuous scene rendered dark, smoky and free of detail so overlay text stays readable, while all faces, flags, weapons and key objects sit entirely within the upper five-eighths. Anything held or carried sits at mid height, never near the bottom edge. Heavy digital oil painting worked over a photographic base, thick visible brushwork and impasto texture on flags, smoke and steel, dramatic hard lighting, heavy atmospheric haze, one dominant colour drowning the frame, every part of the upper five-eighths packed edge to edge with elements and no empty space, cinematic wide-angle, very high contrast, painterly and rough rather than clean — a matte-painting finish, NOT a crisp photo composite, NOT a studio cut-out. Ground eye level, straight down a muddy track so the column walks out toward the camera. The left two-thirds is the disaster: an enormous Russian tricolour of white, blue and red bands strung across the background as heavy cloth with flame eating along its lower edge and the bands still readable, a burning fuel depot behind it, a tank hulk on its side, and a leaning flagpole holding a second scorched tricolour. In front of all of it a column of Russian contract soldiers walks out toward the camera as if none of it were happening, the nearest man waist-up and sharp, the rest progressively smaller and hazier. The right third is divided off by a wall of black smoke: there, chest-up and standing directly in front of the flame, Vladimir Putin (President of Russia) presses his fingertips to his temple with his eyes closed, seen slightly from above and never from below. Deep vertical line between the brows, lips pressed into a hard line, shoulders sunk. Fire puts a red key light on his face so it stays bright against the dark. Orange flame and deep red drown the frame. no text, no letters, no words, no numbers, no captions, no watermark"
    },
